    <h2>The Replacement Wave in the Older Homes</h2>

    <p>A large share of Hartselle's housing stock is old enough that the original or first-replacement systems are aging out all at once. Systems installed in the late 1990s and 2000s are hitting the 15-to-20-year wall, and many of them still run R-22 refrigerant — the stuff that was phased out of production, so topping off a leaky R-22 unit now costs more per pound than the recharge is worth on a system already near the end. For these homeowners the real decision is not repair versus replace so much as when, and the honest trigger is a major-component failure: a dead compressor or a leaking evaporator coil on a 15-plus-year unit is the signal to replace rather than repair.</p>

    <p>The practitioner detail that decides whether the replacement is any good is load calculation. A proper installer runs a Manual J — an actual room-by-room heat-load calc — instead of just matching the tonnage of the old unit. That matters because a lot of the old systems were oversized to begin with, so matching them repeats the mistake. Here is what goes wrong when they skip it: an oversized 4-ton unit on a house that needs 3 tons cools to the setpoint fast, shuts off before it has run long enough to wring the humidity out, and leaves you with a house that is cold and clammy and short-cycling itself toward an early death. A right-sized system runs longer, gentler cycles and actually dehumidifies.</p>

    <h2>Custom Installs in the New Subdivisions</h2>

    <p>The other market is the newer construction — the larger homes in places like Bethel Ridge and the growing edges around Tanner Heights, where the work is a full custom install rather than a swap. These jobs reward a contractor who thinks about the whole system: right-sized equipment, sealed and correctly sized ductwork, a return-air path that can actually feed the blower, and zoning on a two-story so the upstairs is not ten degrees warmer than the down. On a large custom home run hard, a variable-speed system in the 18-plus SEER2 range starts to earn its premium — the same upgrade that would take too many years to pay back on a modest 1,800-square-foot house.</p>

    <p>Both markets share one customer type worth naming: the commuter family that just closed on a house, drives I-65 to Cummings Research Park or Decatur every day, and has not picked an HVAC company yet. When their system quits on the first 95-degree afternoon, they open a browser and start reading. The contractor who has explained this work clearly online is the one they call — not the one with the biggest yellow-pages ad.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What HVAC Work Costs in Hartselle</h2>

    <p>Installed ranges, and what moves them:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Service call / no-cool diagnostic</strong> — about $85 to $150 to show up and find the fault.</li>
      <li><strong>2.5- to 3-ton system replacement</strong> — roughly $7,000 to $11,000 for a standard swap in an existing home.</li>
      <li><strong>4- to 5-ton or ductwork-reworked install</strong> — about $12,000 to $18,000, with the ducts and line set, not the box, driving the number.</li>
      <li><strong>Full custom install with zoning</strong> — a larger new-construction system with proper zoning can run higher still; get the Manual J in writing.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The honest admission most homeowners never hear: two quotes for the same house can be thousands apart and the cheaper one is sometimes the worse deal, because it skipped the load calc and the duct sealing that make the system last. Ask both bidders for the Manual J.</p>

    <h2>When to Do It</h2>

    <p>Replace on your schedule, not the system's. Spring and fall are the smart windows — installers are less slammed than during a July heat wave, and you are not making a rushed five-figure decision while your family sweats. If your system is over 15 years old and still on R-22, treat the next significant failure as the moment to plan the replacement rather than sink another repair into it.</p>

    <h2>Questions Hartselle Homeowners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>Repair or replace?</strong></p>
    <p>Past 15 years with a repair over a third of replacement cost, replace — especially an R-22 system with a failed compressor or coil.</p>

    <p><strong>Why is my house humid with the AC on?</strong></p>
    <p>Almost always an oversized unit that short-cycles before it can dehumidify. It comes from skipping the Manual J load calc.</p>

    <p><strong>What SEER should I buy?</strong></p>
    <p>15 to 16 SEER2 fits most Hartselle homes. High-end variable-speed pays off mainly in a large home run hard year-round.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>Roughly $7,000 to $11,000 for a standard swap, $12,000 to $18,000 with ductwork or a bigger system.</p>

